{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9daa7fa2ab590575", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tMAJEPPA: MORPHING AND ASSESSING IN A UNIFIED PIANO\n2\tPERFORMANCE SPACE\n3\tJinwen Zhou1\n4\tHuan Zhang1\n5\tWeixi Zhai2\n6\tJinhua Liang1\n7\tAidan O. T. Hogg1\n8\tSimon Dixon1\n9\t1\n10\tQueen Mary University of London, UK\n11\t2\n12\tQuanzhou Normal University, China\n13\t[jinwen.zhou, huan.zhang, jinhua.liang, a.hogg, s.e.dixon]@qmul.ac.uk1 , wishzhai@gmail.com2\n14\t\n15\tarXiv:2608.11026v1 [eess.AS] 11 Aug 2026\n16\t\n17\tABSTRACT\n18\tWe present MAJEPPA, a self-supervised framework to learn\n19\tpiano performance representations that span the full skill\n20\tspectrum, from beginner practice sessions to virtuoso concert recordings. We curate the MAJEPPA dataset, comprising ∼4,000 annotated recordings across six expertise\n21\tlevels and six recording contexts. We adapt a single pretrained MIDI autoregressive model with a joint objective:\n22\tnext-token prediction learns score-conditioned performance\n23\tgeneration at various skill levels, while InfoNCE and supervised contrastive losses align abstract score and performance representations in a joint embedding space. The\n24\tproposed model both generates and understands performances in a unified framework. By introducing the EVPMR\n25\tbenchmark, a suite of downstream tasks spanning quality\n26\tassessment, competition ranking, mistake and technique\n27\tclassification, we evaluate the learnt representations, demonstrating progress towards a real-world model for the piano\n28\tperformance space.\n29\t1. INTRODUCTION\n30\tPiano performance is a remarkably rich domain of human\n31\texpression. The same score can be realised as a child’s halting first attempt, an adult student sight-reading, a teacher\n32\tdemonstrating phrasing at half tempo, or a virtuoso commanding the concert stage. Understanding this variation\n33\tcomputationally, distinguishing not just what is played but\n34\thow, is central to music education, where intelligent systems must diagnose mistakes [1–3], assess quality [4, 5],\n35\tprovide formative feedback [6], estimate difficulty [7, 8],\n36\tand personalise learning [9] across student abilities. Recent efforts have begun capturing the learning process itself\n37\tthrough rehearsal datasets [10] and multimodal practice\n38\trecordings [11, 12]. Yet existing approaches address these\n39\tgoals in isolation: task-specific models are trained on narrow datasets for individual objectives, yielding representations that do not transfer across tasks or skill levels.\n40\t© J. Zhou, H. Zhang, W. Zhai, J. Liang, A. O. T. Hogg, and S.\n41\tDixon. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\n42\tLicense (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: J. Zhou, H. Zhang, W. Zhai, J. Liang,\n43\tA. O. T. Hogg, and S. Dixon, “MAJEPPA: Morphing and Assessing in a\n44\tUnified Piano Performance Space”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for\n45\tMusic Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n46\t\n47\tWe argue that a unified representation of piano performance is more foundational, encoding core performance\n48\tparameters from which higher-level structure is derived,\n49\tand more useful in practice. However, building such a representation is hindered by two gaps in MIR research. First,\n50\ta data gap: existing symbolic music datasets focus almost\n51\texclusively on professional performances [13, 14], leaving most real-world piano playing (practice, sight-reading,\n52\tdemonstrations) unrepresented. Second, a methodological\n53\tgap: we need a learning framework that captures abstract\n54\tperformance characteristics (style, accuracy, expressivity)\n55\talongside and linked to the score. We address this gap with\n56\tthe Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) [15], a\n57\tframework that learns representations by predicting in latent space, focusing on high-level semantics. Furthermore,\n58\tLLM-JEPA [16] demonstrates that predictive latent modelling with language models can yield representations that\n59\tare not only abstract but also perceptually grounded.\n60\tGiven a score, we want to predict the abstract character of how that score will be realised. The score serves\n61\tas the context view (specifying what to play) and the performance as the target view (capturing how it is played).\n62\tThe model must predict the abstract performance embedding from the score, along with descriptive conditioning\n63\t(performer type, recording context), without observing the\n64\tperformance tokens. A joint generative objective prevents\n65\trepresentation collapse and retains the model’s ability to\n66\tgenerate, resulting in a single model that both produces\n67\tperformances and yields transferable embeddings that specialise in understanding real-world performances.\n68\tTo close the data gap, we curate the MAJEPPA dataset,\n69\tcomprising ∼4,000 annotated piano recordings spanning\n70\tsix expertise levels and six recording contexts. We evaluate our JEPA-based model on the Evaluation of Piano\n71\tMIDI Representations (EVPMR) benchmark using frozen\n72\trepresentations with linear probes. Our contributions are:\n73\t1. The MAJEPPA dataset, the first large-scale dataset\n74\tto span the full performance spectrum from child\n75\tbeginners to virtuosi;\n76\t2. The first JEPA to model for joint symbolic pianoperformance generation and representation learning,\n77\tusing score–performance pairs as a natural two-view\n78\tstructure;\n79\t3. The EVPMR benchmark suite, designed for evalua-\n80\t\n81\t\f2. RELATED WORK\n82\t\n83\tQuery\n84\t\n85\tEntry Catalogue\n86\t\n87\tATEPP\n88\tAlexander\n89\tScriabin\n90\t12 Etudes, Op.8,\n91\t…\n92\t\n93\tMethod Book Piece +\n94\t‘practice’\n95\t‘Sight-reading’\n96\t‘progress’\n97\t\n98\tWolfgang\n99\tAmadeus\n100\tMozart Piano\n101\tSonata\n102\t…\n103\t\n104\tPlayed by? Teacher / Virtuoso / …\n105\tVideo Type? Practice / Sight-reading / …\n106\tYes / No\n107\tSpeech?\n108\tMetronome? Yes / No\n109\t\n110\t2.1 Music and MIDI representation learning\n111\tApproaches to learning representations from symbolic music are shaped by how MIDI is serialised into a sequence,\n112\tand tokenisation schemes such as REMI [17], Compound\n113\tWord [18], OctupleMIDI [19], and PerTok [20] provide the\n114\tinput vocabularies on top of which the following families\n115\tof self-supervised objectives are applied.\n116\tMasked-reconstruction pre-training: MusicBERT [19]\n117\tand MidiBERT-Piano [21] apply BERT-style masked token\n118\tprediction to symbolic music, producing representations\n119\tthat transfer to composition-level classification (genre, emotion, melody extraction). PianoBART [22] extends this\n120\twith BART-style denoising and multi-level masking over\n121\tOctuple-encoded piano MIDI, unifying understanding and\n122\tgeneration in a single encoder-decoder.\n123\tAutoregressive foundation models: A parallel line of\n124\twork treats MIDI as a language and trains large causal\n125\ttransformers. ChatMusician [23] and MuseCoco [24] target\n126\tcontrollable composition from text, while Moonbeam [25]\n127\tscales next-token prediction to 18 B MIDI tokens, pretraining on ∼81,600 hours of data using a Fundamental\n128\tMusic Embedding tokenizer and multidimensional relative\n129\tattention. Aria [26] focuses on solo piano, pre-training on\n130\t∼60,000 hours of MIDI and obtaining strong embeddings\n131\tvia subsequent contrastive fine-tuning.\n132\tContrastive and cross-modal alignment: CLaMP 3 [27]\n133\taligns multilingual text, symbolic music (ABC and MIDI),\n134\tand audio in a shared embedding space through contrastive\n135\tlearning, supporting retrieval across modality pairs that\n136\twere never jointly observed during training. In the pianoperformance setting, Pianist Transformer [28] removes the\n137\tneed for score-performance alignment and pre-trains on\n138\tlarge unaligned corpora for expressive rendering.\n139\tDownstream evaluation of these representations has centred on composition-level classification (genre, emotion,\n140\tchord recognition). Performance-level understanding, in\n141\tcontrast, remains fragmented across isolated studies, including difficulty estimation [29], pianist identification [30], and\n142\tscore-informed mistake detection [31]. Building on these\n143\tdownstream evaluation tasks and inspired by the Evaluation Package for Audio Representations (EVAR), the unified\n144\tEVPMR benchmark is introduced in this work.\n145\t2.2 Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA)\n146\tJEPA [15] learns representations by predicting target latent embeddings from partial context without reconstruction or contrastive objectives. Using an asymmetric encoder–predictor design, it focuses on modelling predictable\n147\tstructure in latent space, enabling abstract and semantically\n148\tmeaningful representations across domains. This framework has been used to learn semantic image representations\n149\t\n150\t…\n151\t\n152\ttion across downstream tasks, including Chopin competition ranking, technique detection, performance\n153\tquality regression, and conspicuous mistake prediction, where our models outperform various baselines.\n154\t\n155\tSubsequence DTW\n156\tMIDI\n157\tTokenizations\n158\t<pitch> C7\n159\t<pitch> A6\n160\t<onset> 177.242 <onset> 177.501\n161\t<offset> 177.501 <offset> 177.759\n162\t<velocity> 127\n163\t<velocity> 127\n164\tScore_1\n165\t\n166\tScore_2\n167\t\n168\tScore_n\n169\t\n170\t<performer>\n171\tChild beginner\n172\t<video type>\n173\tpractice\n174\t\n175\tCOND_X\n176\t\n177\tCOND_Y\n178\t\n179\t<pitch> A6\n180\t<pitch> C7\n181\t<onset> 275.777 <onset> 276.009\n182\t<offset> 276.049 <offset> 276.259\n183\t<velocity> 70\n184\t<velocity> 69\n185\tPRED\n186\t\n187\tPerf_1\n188\t\n189\tPerf_m\n190\t\n191\tEOS\n192\t\n193\tFigure 1. Data curation pipeline. YouTube recordings\n194\tare collected from entry catalogues and search queries, annotated with performer expertise and recording context,\n195\taligned with the corresponding score via subsequence DTW.\n196\tAligned pairs are segmented and tokenised for pre-training.\n197\t\n198\tby predicting masked spatial target blocks from context\n199\t(I-JEPA [32]), and spatio-temporal representations by predicting latent targets from video context (V-JEPA 2 [33]).\n200\tAudio-JEPA [34] translates the JEPA principle to the audio domain by predicting latent representations of masked\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6794b662-7f11-40e1-a8da-8cda3a766949", "uuid": "9bc403af-0981-4ac2-b08d-2f098d844b4b", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T09:26:31.222Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/majeppa.txt", "content": "MAJEPPA: MORPHING AND ASSESSING IN A UNIFIED PIANO\nPERFORMANCE SPACE\nJinwen Zhou1\nHuan Zhang1\nWeixi Zhai2\nJinhua Liang1\nAidan O. T. Hogg1\nSimon Dixon1\n1\nQueen Mary University of London, UK\n2\nQuanzhou Normal University, China\n[jinwen.zhou, huan.zhang, jinhua.liang, a.hogg, s.e.dixon]@qmul.ac.uk1 , wishzhai@gmail.com2\n\narXiv:2608.11026v1 [eess.AS] 11 Aug 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nWe present MAJEPPA, a self-supervised framework to learn\npiano performance representations that span the full skill\nspectrum, from beginner practice sessions to virtuoso concert recordings. We curate the MAJEPPA dataset, comprising ∼4,000 annotated recordings across six expertise\nlevels and six recording contexts. We adapt a single pretrained MIDI autoregressive model with a joint objective:\nnext-token prediction learns score-conditioned performance\ngeneration at various skill levels, while InfoNCE and supervised contrastive losses align abstract score and performance representations in a joint embedding space. The\nproposed model both generates and understands performances in a unified framework. By introducing the EVPMR\nbenchmark, a suite of downstream tasks spanning quality\nassessment, competition ranking, mistake and technique\nclassification, we evaluate the learnt representations, demonstrating progress towards a real-world model for the piano\nperformance space.\n1. INTRODUCTION\nPiano performance is a remarkably rich domain of human\nexpression. The same score can be realised as a child’s halting first attempt, an adult student sight-reading, a teacher\ndemonstrating phrasing at half tempo, or a virtuoso commanding the concert stage. Understanding this variation\ncomputationally, distinguishing not just what is played but\nhow, is central to music education, where intelligent systems must diagnose mistakes [1–3], assess quality [4, 5],\nprovide formative feedback [6], estimate difficulty [7, 8],\nand personalise learning [9] across student abilities. Recent efforts have begun capturing the learning process itself\nthrough rehearsal datasets [10] and multimodal practice\nrecordings [11, 12]. Yet existing approaches address these\ngoals in isolation: task-specific models are trained on narrow datasets for individual objectives, yielding representations that do not transfer across tasks or skill levels.\n© J. Zhou, H. Zhang, W. Zhai, J. Liang, A. O. T. Hogg, and S.\nDixon. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\nLicense (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: J. Zhou, H. Zhang, W. Zhai, J. Liang,\nA. O. T. Hogg, and S. Dixon, “MAJEPPA: Morphing and Assessing in a\nUnified Piano Performance Space”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n\nWe argue that a unified representation of piano performance is more foundational, encoding core performance\nparameters from which higher-level structure is derived,\nand more useful in practice. However, building such a representation is hindered by two gaps in MIR research. First,\na data gap: existing symbolic music datasets focus almost\nexclusively on professional performances [13, 14], leaving most real-world piano playing (practice, sight-reading,\ndemonstrations) unrepresented. Second, a methodological\ngap: we need a learning framework that captures abstract\nperformance characteristics (style, accuracy, expressivity)\nalongside and linked to the score. We address this gap with\nthe Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) [15], a\nframework that learns representations by predicting in latent space, focusing on high-level semantics. Furthermore,\nLLM-JEPA [16] demonstrates that predictive latent modelling with language models can yield representations that\nare not only abstract but also perceptually grounded.\nGiven a score, we want to predict the abstract character of how that score will be realised. The score serves\nas the context view (specifying what to play) and the performance as the target view (capturing how it is played).\nThe model must predict the abstract performance embedding from the score, along with descriptive conditioning\n(performer type, recording context), without observing the\nperformance tokens. A joint generative objective prevents\nrepresentation collapse and retains the model’s ability to\ngenerate, resulting in a single model that both produces\nperformances and yields transferable embeddings that specialise in understanding real-world performances.\nTo close the data gap, we curate the MAJEPPA dataset,\ncomprising ∼4,000 annotated piano recordings spanning\nsix expertise levels and six recording contexts. We evaluate our JEPA-based model on the Evaluation of Piano\nMIDI Representations (EVPMR) benchmark using frozen\nrepresentations with linear probes. Our contributions are:\n1. The MAJEPPA dataset, the first large-scale dataset\nto span the full performance spectrum from child\nbeginners to virtuosi;\n2. The first JEPA to model for joint symbolic pianoperformance generation and representation learning,\nusing score–performance pairs as a natural two-view\nstructure;\n3. The EVPMR benchmark suite, designed for evalua-\n\n\f2. RELATED WORK\n\nQuery\n\nEntry Catalogue\n\nATEPP\nAlexander\nScriabin\n12 Etudes, Op.8,\n…\n\nMethod Book Piece +\n‘practice’\n‘Sight-reading’\n‘progress’\n\nWolfgang\nAmadeus\nMozart Piano\nSonata\n…\n\nPlayed by? Teacher / Virtuoso / …\nVideo Type? Practice / Sight-reading / …\nYes / No\nSpeech?\nMetronome? Yes / No\n\n2.1 Music and MIDI representation learning\nApproaches to learning representations from symbolic music are shaped by how MIDI is serialised into a sequence,\nand tokenisation schemes such as REMI [17], Compound\nWord [18], OctupleMIDI [19], and PerTok [20] provide the\ninput vocabularies on top of which the following families\nof self-supervised objectives are applied.\nMasked-reconstruction pre-training: MusicBERT [19]\nand MidiBERT-Piano [21] apply BERT-style masked token\nprediction to symbolic music, producing representations\nthat transfer to composition-level classification (genre, emotion, melody extraction). PianoBART [22] extends this\nwith BART-style denoising and multi-level masking over\nOctuple-encoded piano MIDI, unifying understanding and\ngeneration in a single encoder-decoder.\nAutoregressive foundation models: A parallel line of\nwork treats MIDI as a language and trains large causal\ntransformers. ChatMusician [23] and MuseCoco [24] target\ncontrollable composition from text, while Moonbeam [25]\nscales next-token prediction to 18 B MIDI tokens, pretraining on ∼81,600 hours of data using a Fundamental\nMusic Embedding tokenizer and multidimensional relative\nattention. Aria [26] focuses on solo piano, pre-training on\n∼60,000 hours of MIDI and obtaining strong embeddings\nvia subsequent contrastive fine-tuning.\nContrastive and cross-modal alignment: CLaMP 3 [27]\naligns multilingual text, symbolic music (ABC and MIDI),\nand audio in a shared embedding space through contrastive\nlearning, supporting retrieval across modality pairs that\nwere never jointly observed during training. In the pianoperformance setting, Pianist Transformer [28] removes the\nneed for score-performance alignment and pre-trains on\nlarge unaligned corpora for expressive rendering.\nDownstream evaluation of these representations has centred on composition-level classification (genre, emotion,\nchord recognition). Performance-level understanding, in\ncontrast, remains fragmented across isolated studies, including difficulty estimation [29], pianist identification [30], and\nscore-informed mistake detection [31]. Building on these\ndownstream evaluation tasks and inspired by the Evaluation Package for Audio Representations (EVAR), the unified\nEVPMR benchmark is introduced in this work.\n2.2 Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA)\nJEPA [15] learns representations by predicting target latent embeddings from partial context without reconstruction or contrastive objectives. Using an asymmetric encoder–predictor design, it focuses on modelling predictable\nstructure in latent space, enabling abstract and semantically\nmeaningful representations across domains. This framework has been used to learn semantic image representations\n\n…\n\ntion across downstream tasks, including Chopin competition ranking, technique detection, performance\nquality regression, and conspicuous mistake prediction, where our models outperform various baselines.\n\nSubsequence DTW\nMIDI\nTokenizations\n<pitch> C7\n<pitch> A6\n<onset> 177.242 <onset> 177.501\n<offset> 177.501 <offset> 177.759\n<velocity> 127\n<velocity> 127\nScore_1\n\nScore_2\n\nScore_n\n\n<performer>\nChild beginner\n<video type>\npractice\n\nCOND_X\n\nCOND_Y\n\n<pitch> A6\n<pitch> C7\n<onset> 275.777 <onset> 276.009\n<offset> 276.049 <offset> 276.259\n<velocity> 70\n<velocity> 69\nPRED\n\nPerf_1\n\nPerf_m\n\nEOS\n\nFigure 1. Data curation pipeline. YouTube recordings\nare collected from entry catalogues and search queries, annotated with performer expertise and recording context,\naligned with the corresponding score via subsequence DTW.\nAligned pairs are segmented and tokenised for pre-training.\n\nby predicting masked spatial target blocks from context\n(I-JEPA [32]), and spatio-temporal representations by predicting latent targets from video context (V-JEPA 2 [33]).\nAudio-JEPA [34] translates the JEPA principle to the audio domain by predicting latent representations of masked", "numLines": 200, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 946}}}
