{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d386fe3d445cb23", "type": "tool_result", "content": "200\tAudio-JEPA [34] translates the JEPA principle to the audio domain by predicting latent representations of masked\n201\tmel-spectrogram patches.\n202\tIn the music domain, Stem-JEPA [35] leverages the\n203\tmulti-track structure of music to replace input-space masking with stem holdout, training the predictor to predict the\n204\tlatent representation of a missing instrument from a context mix conditioned on instrument labels. JEP-AGA [36]\n205\tcombines the principles of JEPA with autoregressive generation for music infilling. AMT-JEPA [37] demonstrates that\n206\tJEPA can learn semantically meaningful representations\n207\tfor multi-instrument music transcription, particularly capturing instrument and pitch-related information. Hachana\n208\tand Rasheed [38] extend JEPA to symbolic domain with\n209\tmusic-specific masking strategies for MIDI sequences.\n210\t3. DATA CURATION\n211\tExisting symbolic music datasets focus predominantly on\n212\tprofessional performances: ATEPP [13] contains virtuoso\n213\tinterpretations and ASAP [14] provides competition-level\n214\trecordings with aligned scores. To address this gap, we curate the MAJEPPA dataset, comprising 3,979 real-world\n215\tpiano recordings across 942 distinct pieces and movements,\n216\tspanning the full performance spectrum from child beginners to concert virtuosi (Figure 2).\n217\tCollection: Solo-piano recordings that are publicly available are collected from YouTube, covering demo recordings,\n218\tinformal practice, and sight-reading sessions. To ensure\n219\tdiversity of performance levels across the music pieces,\n220\t\n221\t\fVirtuoso Concert Teacher\n222\tPerformance\n223\tDemo Play\n224\t\n225\tStudent (child)\n226\tHome Practice\n227\t\n228\tStudent (adult)\n229\tSight Reading\n230\t\n231\tTable 1. Distribution of recordings by performer expertise\n232\t(rows) and recording context (columns).\n233\tPrac. Sight. Show. Demo Slow Conc. Total Hours\n234\t\n235\tFigure 2. The same piece realised across different expertise\n236\tlevels and recording contexts. Left: score excerpt with\n237\tMIDI piano roll. Right: four performances showing visible\n238\tdifferences in timing, note accuracy, and dynamics.\n239\twe source our data from catalogues and channels (top of\n240\tFigure 1). Firstly, we query for repertoire titles, adding\n241\tkeywords such as Practice, Sight-reading, or Progress. For\n242\tconcert-repertoire level pieces, we query for videos of the\n243\tsame pieces or movements as those in ATEPP. To include\n244\tbeginner-level material, we query for exercises and pedagogical repertoire commonly used in piano training, such\n245\tas method books by Carl Czerny, Friedrich Burgmüller, and\n246\tAnton Diabelli. We also include widely practised contemporary pieces such as Kiss the Rain by Yiruma. From the\n247\tresults, we manually identify resourceful channels from\n248\tpiano teachers (demonstrations and tutorials), sight-reading\n249\tsessions, practice recordings, and learning progress videos.\n250\t3.1 Annotation\n251\tAfter sourcing videos, each recording is manually annotated by conservatoire student pianists 1 who work from\n252\ta shared calibration document that defines each category\n253\tand provides reference clips. The annotations capture differences along two orthogonal axes: recording context and\n254\tperformer expertise (Table 1). Recording context describes\n255\tthe setting in which the performance was captured:\n256\t• Practice: Recordings from students who are actively\n257\tlearning a particular piece.\n258\t• Sight-reading: Sight-reading sessions involving one\n259\tor multiple unfamiliar pieces.\n260\t• Showcase: Recordings presented to teachers (e.g.,\n261\tprior to weekly lessons) or in student concerts, reflecting a more polished, near-standard performance\n262\twith relatively fewer mistakes despite the performer’s\n263\tdeveloping skill level.\n264\t• Demo: Demonstration recordings by piano teachers\n265\tillustrating how a piece should be performed. They\n266\tare usually highly accurate in terms of pitch and timing, and not overly expressive.\n267\t• Slow demo: Slow-tempo demonstration recordings\n268\tby piano teachers.\n269\t• Concert: Commercial recordings or live performances from pianists.\n270\tPerformer expertise describes the background of the\n271\tperformer:\n272\t• Child beginner: Young learners performing simple\n273\texercises or early-stage repertoire.\n274\t1 Annotator reliability: https://jepa-demo.vercel.app\n275\t\n276\tTeacher\n277\tAd. Beg.\n278\tAd. Int.\n279\tCh. Beg.\n280\tCh. Pro.\n281\tVirtuoso\n282\t\n283\t35\n284\t622\n285\t444\n286\t186\n287\t84\n288\t0\n289\t\n290\t15\n291\t217\n292\t135\n293\t8\n294\t0\n295\t0\n296\t\n297\t55\n298\t92\n299\t177\n300\t193\n301\t161\n302\t0\n303\t\n304\t690\n305\t12\n306\t16\n307\t2\n308\t2\n309\t0\n310\t\n311\t311\n312\t3\n313\t2\n314\t2\n315\t0\n316\t0\n317\t\n318\t173 1279\n319\t1 947\n320\t3 777\n321\t0 391\n322\t0 247\n323\t338 338\n324\t\n325\t76.1\n326\t127.9\n327\t50.7\n328\t8.9\n329\t13.7\n330\t28.9\n331\t\n332\tTotal\n333\t\n334\t1371\n335\t\n336\t375\n337\t\n338\t678\n339\t\n340\t722\n341\t\n342\t318\n343\t\n344\t515 3979\n345\t\n346\t303.2\n347\t\n348\t• Child professional: Young performers demonstrating advanced technical proficiency, typically on a\n349\tpre-professional or professional training track.\n350\t• Adult beginner: Adult learners performing introductory exercises or beginner repertoire, typically\n351\tengaging with piano as a hobby.\n352\t• Adult intermediate: Performers demonstrating moderate technical proficiency and developing musicality.\n353\t• Piano teacher: Instructors providing demonstration\n354\tperformances for instructional purposes.\n355\t• Virtuoso: Established professional pianists.\n356\tBesides the conditioning tags, the annotators also provide the additional information: 1). Breakpoints for long\n357\tvideos, especially when multiple pieces are in a single\n358\trecording; 2). Corresponding scores sourced from MuseScore for each identified piece, with annotators visually\n359\tmatching the score edition to the performed arrangement\n360\t(e.g., key, structure, and approximate length); 3). Filtering\n361\tout videos that contain speech, metronome, or other nonpiano sounds, and those not meeting the dataset criteria.\n362\t3.2 Processing\n363\tThe final processing pipeline produces aligned pairs of score\n364\tand performance MIDI segments: 1). Breakpoint processing: long recordings are first segmented into individual\n365\tperformance excerpts based on annotation. Each segment is\n366\tassociated with its corresponding score; 2). Transcription:\n367\tperformance audio is transcribed into MIDI using Transkun [39], chosen as the note-level state of the art on standard piano AMT benchmarks rather than alternatives such\n368\tas Aria-AMT [40], though we did not explicitly check transcription quality (including pedal artefacts); 3). Alignment:\n369\tto enable segment-level pairing under the limited context\n370\tlength of language models, score and performance must be\n371\ttemporally aligned before cropping. Due to the highly nonideal and fuzzy nature of real-world data (e.g., mistakes,\n372\tomitted passages, one-hand practice), we perform subsequence dynamic time warping (DTW) on chroma features\n373\textracted from synthesised score audio and performance audio, allowing robust partial alignment. Subsequence DTW\n374\thandles omitted repeats by aligning the performance to the\n375\trelevant contiguous score portion; performances with extra\n376\trepetitions may produce locally noisier pairs, which the\n377\tsegment-level objective tolerates. The resulting warping\n378\tpath maps score time to performance time, enabling consistent segmentation into fixed-token-length windows for\n379\t\n380\t\fFigure 3. Sequence packing and attention mask design. Left: standard causal mask for the generative item. Middle:\n381\tblock-diagonal causal mask for the JEPA item, enforcing independent encoding of score and performance views. Right:\n382\tcontrastive objectives for piece alignment, cross-condition clustering, and within-performance alignment.\n383\tdownstream processing.\n384\t4. PROPOSED METHOD\n385\tOur goal is to learn a unified representation of piano performance that captures both the underlying musical content\n386\tand its expressive realisation. To this end, we model score\n387\tand performance as two complementary views of the same\n388\tmusical excerpt, and learn to predict performance representations from score representations in a shared latent space.\n389\tWe formulate each training example as a score–\n390\tperformance pair (s, p) corresponding to the same musical\n391\texcerpt. The score view s is a deadpan tokenisation of the\n392\twritten score, while the performance view p is the tokenised\n393\texpressive realisation of the same music. Both share the\n394\tsame musical content, but differ in the performance detail:\n395\tthe score specifies the intended notes and structure, whereas\n396\tthe performance carries timing, dynamics, articulation, and\n397\trecording context. Following LLM-JEPA [16], we treat this\n398\tpair as a natural two-view structure and train a predictor\n399\tto map from the score view to the performance view in\n400\trepresentation space. A mini-batch contains B such pairs.\n401\tThe objective is to learn a unified representation of piano\n402\tperformance that supports both conditional generation and\n403\tabstract embedding prediction. Specifically, the model is\n404\ttrained to (i) generate performance tokens conditioned on\n405\tscore tokens, and (ii) predict a latent representation of the\n406\tperformance from the score and conditioning tokens alone.\n407\t4.1 Model components\n408\tWe implement this two-view predictive framework within\n409\ta single autoregressive transformer by augmenting it with\n410\tspecialised tokens and projection heads. The architecture\n411\tcomprises an encoder backbone, a predictor via [PRED]\n412\ttokens, conditioning tokens encoding performer and recording context, and a projection head for contrastive objectives.\n413\tEncoder. We use Aria-medium [26], a 632M-parameter\n414\tLLaMA-style autoregressive transformer pre-trained on\n415\t\n416\tsymbolic MIDI. Predictor. We append k [PRED] tokens\n417\tto the input sequence and reuse the model’s self-attention as\n418\ta tied-weights predictor: the final [PRED] state represents\n419\tPred(s). Condition tokens. Two tokens, [COND_X] (performer) and [COND_Y] (recording context), provide categorical conditioning via learned embeddings (Section 3).\n420\t4.2 Sequence construction and attention masking\n421\tEach score–performance pair (s, p) is first packed\n422\tinto a single token sequence as shown in Fig. 3:\n423\t[s; COND; PRED; p; EOS], where s denotes the score\n424\ttokens, p the performance tokens, and COND, PRED, and\n425\tEOS are special tokens marking the conditioning boundary,\n426\tprediction query, and sequence termination, respectively.\n427\tPass 1 – generative (causal mask). The packed sequence [s; COND; PRED; p; EOS] is processed under a\n428\tstandard lower-triangular causal mask, so performance tokens attend to the full preceding context. This pass produces\n429\tthe next-token logits over the performance tokens used by\n430\tthe generative loss Lgen defined below.\n431\tPass 2 – JEPA (block-diagonal causal mask). The\n432\tsame tokens are processed under a block-diagonal mask that\n433\tdefines two independent views: View 1 [s; COND; PRED]\n434\tand View 2 [p; EOS], each internally causal but mutually\n435\tinvisible. We extract Pred(s) from the [PRED] position\n436\tin View 1 and Enc(p) from the [EOS] position in View 2.\n437\tBy construction (Fig. 3), [PRED] attends only to score and\n438\tconditioning tokens.\n439\tThis split ensures (i) Pass 1 retains the model’s ability to\n440\tgenerate performances conditioned on scores, and (ii) Pass 2\n441\tmust form an abstract prediction from the score alone, since\n442\t[PRED] never observes the performance tokens.\n443\t4.3 Training objective\n444\tThe training loss combines a generative term with contrastive terms that align score and performance representations, enforce condition-aware structure, and maintain\n445\t\n446\t\fwithin-performance consistency. For each pair i in the\n447\tbatch, we obtain two embeddings via a shared linear projection head ϕ : R1536 → R512 , both ℓ2 -normalised:\n448\tzsi = ϕ(Pred(si ))\n449\t\n450\tand\n451\t\n452\tzpi = ϕ(Enc(pi )),\n453\t\n454\twhere Pred(si ) and Enc(pi ) are the Pass 2 outputs from\n455\tthe [PRED] and [EOS] positions respectively. The training loss combines a generative term computed from Pass 1\n456\twith three contrastive JEPA terms computed from Pass 2:\n457\t\u0001\n458\tL = γ Lgen + λ Lpiece + β Lcond + α Lself , (1)\n459\twhere the four loss terms are defined below and γ, λ, β, α ≥\n460\t0 are scalar weights. All contrastive terms share a single\n461\ttemperature τ .\n462\tGenerative loss Lgen is the standard next-token crossentropy over the performance tokens of p, conditioned on\n463\tscore and conditioning tokens through the Pass 1 causal\n464\tmask.\n465\tPiece alignment Lpiece is an InfoNCE loss that pulls\n466\teach predicted score embedding towards its paired performance embedding within the batch:\n467\tB\n468\t\n469\tLpiece = −\n470\t\n471\texp(zsi · zpi /τ )\n472\t1 X\n473\tlog PB\n474\t.\n475\tp\n476\ts\n477\tB i=1\n478\tj=1 exp(zi · zj /τ )\n479\t\n480\t(2)\n481\t\n482\tCross-condition clustering Lcond is a soft supervised\n483\tcontrastive loss over performance embeddings. Let wij ∈\n484\t[0, 1] denote a continuous similarity between samples i and\n485\tj, derived from the ordinal distance between their performer\n486\tskill levels and recording formality levels, and let Ci =\n487\t{j : cond(j) ̸= cond(i)} restrict to pairs with different\n488\tcondition tags:\n489\tLcond = −\n490\t\n491\tB\n492\texp(zpi · zpj /τ )\n493\t1 XX\n494\t,\n495\tw̄ij log P\n496\tp\n497\tp\n498\tB i=1\n499\th∈Ci exp(zi · zh /τ )\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6794b662-7f11-40e1-a8da-8cda3a766949", "uuid": "b38176fe-d291-4af6-bb25-7fab8dafe4ed", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T09:26:33.333Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/majeppa.txt", "content": "Audio-JEPA [34] translates the JEPA principle to the audio domain by predicting latent representations of masked\nmel-spectrogram patches.\nIn the music domain, Stem-JEPA [35] leverages the\nmulti-track structure of music to replace input-space masking with stem holdout, training the predictor to predict the\nlatent representation of a missing instrument from a context mix conditioned on instrument labels. JEP-AGA [36]\ncombines the principles of JEPA with autoregressive generation for music infilling. AMT-JEPA [37] demonstrates that\nJEPA can learn semantically meaningful representations\nfor multi-instrument music transcription, particularly capturing instrument and pitch-related information. Hachana\nand Rasheed [38] extend JEPA to symbolic domain with\nmusic-specific masking strategies for MIDI sequences.\n3. DATA CURATION\nExisting symbolic music datasets focus predominantly on\nprofessional performances: ATEPP [13] contains virtuoso\ninterpretations and ASAP [14] provides competition-level\nrecordings with aligned scores. To address this gap, we curate the MAJEPPA dataset, comprising 3,979 real-world\npiano recordings across 942 distinct pieces and movements,\nspanning the full performance spectrum from child beginners to concert virtuosi (Figure 2).\nCollection: Solo-piano recordings that are publicly available are collected from YouTube, covering demo recordings,\ninformal practice, and sight-reading sessions. To ensure\ndiversity of performance levels across the music pieces,\n\n\fVirtuoso Concert Teacher\nPerformance\nDemo Play\n\nStudent (child)\nHome Practice\n\nStudent (adult)\nSight Reading\n\nTable 1. Distribution of recordings by performer expertise\n(rows) and recording context (columns).\nPrac. Sight. Show. Demo Slow Conc. Total Hours\n\nFigure 2. The same piece realised across different expertise\nlevels and recording contexts. Left: score excerpt with\nMIDI piano roll. Right: four performances showing visible\ndifferences in timing, note accuracy, and dynamics.\nwe source our data from catalogues and channels (top of\nFigure 1). Firstly, we query for repertoire titles, adding\nkeywords such as Practice, Sight-reading, or Progress. For\nconcert-repertoire level pieces, we query for videos of the\nsame pieces or movements as those in ATEPP. To include\nbeginner-level material, we query for exercises and pedagogical repertoire commonly used in piano training, such\nas method books by Carl Czerny, Friedrich Burgmüller, and\nAnton Diabelli. We also include widely practised contemporary pieces such as Kiss the Rain by Yiruma. From the\nresults, we manually identify resourceful channels from\npiano teachers (demonstrations and tutorials), sight-reading\nsessions, practice recordings, and learning progress videos.\n3.1 Annotation\nAfter sourcing videos, each recording is manually annotated by conservatoire student pianists 1 who work from\na shared calibration document that defines each category\nand provides reference clips. The annotations capture differences along two orthogonal axes: recording context and\nperformer expertise (Table 1). Recording context describes\nthe setting in which the performance was captured:\n• Practice: Recordings from students who are actively\nlearning a particular piece.\n• Sight-reading: Sight-reading sessions involving one\nor multiple unfamiliar pieces.\n• Showcase: Recordings presented to teachers (e.g.,\nprior to weekly lessons) or in student concerts, reflecting a more polished, near-standard performance\nwith relatively fewer mistakes despite the performer’s\ndeveloping skill level.\n• Demo: Demonstration recordings by piano teachers\nillustrating how a piece should be performed. They\nare usually highly accurate in terms of pitch and timing, and not overly expressive.\n• Slow demo: Slow-tempo demonstration recordings\nby piano teachers.\n• Concert: Commercial recordings or live performances from pianists.\nPerformer expertise describes the background of the\nperformer:\n• Child beginner: Young learners performing simple\nexercises or early-stage repertoire.\n1 Annotator reliability: https://jepa-demo.vercel.app\n\nTeacher\nAd. Beg.\nAd. Int.\nCh. Beg.\nCh. Pro.\nVirtuoso\n\n35\n622\n444\n186\n84\n0\n\n15\n217\n135\n8\n0\n0\n\n55\n92\n177\n193\n161\n0\n\n690\n12\n16\n2\n2\n0\n\n311\n3\n2\n2\n0\n0\n\n173 1279\n1 947\n3 777\n0 391\n0 247\n338 338\n\n76.1\n127.9\n50.7\n8.9\n13.7\n28.9\n\nTotal\n\n1371\n\n375\n\n678\n\n722\n\n318\n\n515 3979\n\n303.2\n\n• Child professional: Young performers demonstrating advanced technical proficiency, typically on a\npre-professional or professional training track.\n• Adult beginner: Adult learners performing introductory exercises or beginner repertoire, typically\nengaging with piano as a hobby.\n• Adult intermediate: Performers demonstrating moderate technical proficiency and developing musicality.\n• Piano teacher: Instructors providing demonstration\nperformances for instructional purposes.\n• Virtuoso: Established professional pianists.\nBesides the conditioning tags, the annotators also provide the additional information: 1). Breakpoints for long\nvideos, especially when multiple pieces are in a single\nrecording; 2). Corresponding scores sourced from MuseScore for each identified piece, with annotators visually\nmatching the score edition to the performed arrangement\n(e.g., key, structure, and approximate length); 3). Filtering\nout videos that contain speech, metronome, or other nonpiano sounds, and those not meeting the dataset criteria.\n3.2 Processing\nThe final processing pipeline produces aligned pairs of score\nand performance MIDI segments: 1). Breakpoint processing: long recordings are first segmented into individual\nperformance excerpts based on annotation. Each segment is\nassociated with its corresponding score; 2). Transcription:\nperformance audio is transcribed into MIDI using Transkun [39], chosen as the note-level state of the art on standard piano AMT benchmarks rather than alternatives such\nas Aria-AMT [40], though we did not explicitly check transcription quality (including pedal artefacts); 3). Alignment:\nto enable segment-level pairing under the limited context\nlength of language models, score and performance must be\ntemporally aligned before cropping. Due to the highly nonideal and fuzzy nature of real-world data (e.g., mistakes,\nomitted passages, one-hand practice), we perform subsequence dynamic time warping (DTW) on chroma features\nextracted from synthesised score audio and performance audio, allowing robust partial alignment. Subsequence DTW\nhandles omitted repeats by aligning the performance to the\nrelevant contiguous score portion; performances with extra\nrepetitions may produce locally noisier pairs, which the\nsegment-level objective tolerates. The resulting warping\npath maps score time to performance time, enabling consistent segmentation into fixed-token-length windows for\n\n\fFigure 3. Sequence packing and attention mask design. Left: standard causal mask for the generative item. Middle:\nblock-diagonal causal mask for the JEPA item, enforcing independent encoding of score and performance views. Right:\ncontrastive objectives for piece alignment, cross-condition clustering, and within-performance alignment.\ndownstream processing.\n4. PROPOSED METHOD\nOur goal is to learn a unified representation of piano performance that captures both the underlying musical content\nand its expressive realisation. To this end, we model score\nand performance as two complementary views of the same\nmusical excerpt, and learn to predict performance representations from score representations in a shared latent space.\nWe formulate each training example as a score–\nperformance pair (s, p) corresponding to the same musical\nexcerpt. The score view s is a deadpan tokenisation of the\nwritten score, while the performance view p is the tokenised\nexpressive realisation of the same music. Both share the\nsame musical content, but differ in the performance detail:\nthe score specifies the intended notes and structure, whereas\nthe performance carries timing, dynamics, articulation, and\nrecording context. Following LLM-JEPA [16], we treat this\npair as a natural two-view structure and train a predictor\nto map from the score view to the performance view in\nrepresentation space. A mini-batch contains B such pairs.\nThe objective is to learn a unified representation of piano\nperformance that supports both conditional generation and\nabstract embedding prediction. Specifically, the model is\ntrained to (i) generate performance tokens conditioned on\nscore tokens, and (ii) predict a latent representation of the\nperformance from the score and conditioning tokens alone.\n4.1 Model components\nWe implement this two-view predictive framework within\na single autoregressive transformer by augmenting it with\nspecialised tokens and projection heads. The architecture\ncomprises an encoder backbone, a predictor via [PRED]\ntokens, conditioning tokens encoding performer and recording context, and a projection head for contrastive objectives.\nEncoder. We use Aria-medium [26], a 632M-parameter\nLLaMA-style autoregressive transformer pre-trained on\n\nsymbolic MIDI. Predictor. We append k [PRED] tokens\nto the input sequence and reuse the model’s self-attention as\na tied-weights predictor: the final [PRED] state represents\nPred(s). Condition tokens. Two tokens, [COND_X] (performer) and [COND_Y] (recording context), provide categorical conditioning via learned embeddings (Section 3).\n4.2 Sequence construction and attention masking\nEach score–performance pair (s, p) is first packed\ninto a single token sequence as shown in Fig. 3:\n[s; COND; PRED; p; EOS], where s denotes the score\ntokens, p the performance tokens, and COND, PRED, and\nEOS are special tokens marking the conditioning boundary,\nprediction query, and sequence termination, respectively.\nPass 1 – generative (causal mask). The packed sequence [s; COND; PRED; p; EOS] is processed under a\nstandard lower-triangular causal mask, so performance tokens attend to the full preceding context. This pass produces\nthe next-token logits over the performance tokens used by\nthe generative loss Lgen defined below.\nPass 2 – JEPA (block-diagonal causal mask). The\nsame tokens are processed under a block-diagonal mask that\ndefines two independent views: View 1 [s; COND; PRED]\nand View 2 [p; EOS], each internally causal but mutually\ninvisible. We extract Pred(s) from the [PRED] position\nin View 1 and Enc(p) from the [EOS] position in View 2.\nBy construction (Fig. 3), [PRED] attends only to score and\nconditioning tokens.\nThis split ensures (i) Pass 1 retains the model’s ability to\ngenerate performances conditioned on scores, and (ii) Pass 2\nmust form an abstract prediction from the score alone, since\n[PRED] never observes the performance tokens.\n4.3 Training objective\nThe training loss combines a generative term with contrastive terms that align score and performance representations, enforce condition-aware structure, and maintain\n\n\fwithin-performance consistency. For each pair i in the\nbatch, we obtain two embeddings via a shared linear projection head ϕ : R1536 → R512 , both ℓ2 -normalised:\nzsi = ϕ(Pred(si ))\n\nand\n\nzpi = ϕ(Enc(pi )),\n\nwhere Pred(si ) and Enc(pi ) are the Pass 2 outputs from\nthe [PRED] and [EOS] positions respectively. The training loss combines a generative term computed from Pass 1\nwith three contrastive JEPA terms computed from Pass 2:\n\u0001\nL = γ Lgen + λ Lpiece + β Lcond + α Lself , (1)\nwhere the four loss terms are defined below and γ, λ, β, α ≥\n0 are scalar weights. All contrastive terms share a single\ntemperature τ .\nGenerative loss Lgen is the standard next-token crossentropy over the performance tokens of p, conditioned on\nscore and conditioning tokens through the Pass 1 causal\nmask.\nPiece alignment Lpiece is an InfoNCE loss that pulls\neach predicted score embedding towards its paired performance embedding within the batch:\nB\n\nLpiece = −\n\nexp(zsi · zpi /τ )\n1 X\nlog PB\n.\np\ns\nB i=1\nj=1 exp(zi · zj /τ )\n\n(2)\n\nCross-condition clustering Lcond is a soft supervised\ncontrastive loss over performance embeddings. Let wij ∈\n[0, 1] denote a continuous similarity between samples i and\nj, derived from the ordinal distance between their performer\nskill levels and recording formality levels, and let Ci =\n{j : cond(j) ̸= cond(i)} restrict to pairs with different\ncondition tags:\nLcond = −\n\nB\nexp(zpi · zpj /τ )\n1 XX\n,\nw̄ij log P\np\np\nB i=1\nh∈Ci exp(zi · zh /τ )", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 200, "totalLines": 946}}}
