{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8bf529ad0c6dbd51", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\tj∈Ci\n501\t\n502\t(3)\n503\tP\n504\twith w̄ij = wij / h∈Ci wih row-normalised over Ci . This\n505\tterm replaces the cosine-distance loss of [16], which we\n506\tfound prone to representation collapse in our setting. See\n507\tour website 1 for details of continuous condition similarity\n508\tdesign.\n509\tWithin-performance alignment Lself is a symmetric\n510\tInfoNCE loss over pairs of segments from the same performance. We form B pairs (pai , pbi ) with embeddings zai , zbi ,\n511\tand define\n512\tLself = −\n513\t\n514\tB\n515\t1 X X\n516\texp(zui ·ziū /τ )\n517\t,\n518\tlog PB\n519\tu ū\n520\tB i=1\n521\tj=1 exp(zi ·zj /τ )\n522\t\n523\t(4)\n524\t\n525\tu∈{a,b}\n526\t\n527\twhere ā = b and b̄ = a. Both terms encourage segments\n528\tfrom the same performance to be more similar than segments from different recordings, while averaging over the\n529\ttwo directions yields a symmetric objective. Since the two\n530\tsegments share performer and recording tags, Lself complements Lcond by encouraging within-performance coherence.\n531\tTraining setup: We freeze the pre-trained Aria weights\n532\tand apply Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [41] to the attention projections mixed_qkv and att_proj_linear\n533\t\n534\t(rank r = 512, scaling αLoRA = 32), giving ∼75M trainable\n535\tparameters out of 660M. The context window is extended\n536\tfrom 2048 to 4096 tokens via linear RoPE scaling [42].\n537\tLoss hyperparameters in Eq. (1) are set to γ = 1, λ = 8,\n538\tβ = 1, α = 1, with temperature τ = 0.07 and k = 1 [PRED]\n539\ttoken, chosen by ablation.\n540\tWe train on 4×NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs in bfloat16\n541\tmixed precision for 120 epochs, with a per-GPU batch size\n542\tof 8 and gradient accumulation of 4. Optimisation uses\n543\tAdamW (weight decay 0.01) with separate learning rates\n544\tfor these LoRA modules (2×10−4 ), the new conditioning\n545\tand [PRED] token embeddings (1×10−3 ), and the LM\n546\thead (1×10−5 ).\n547\t5. DOWNSTREAM TASKS AND EVALUATION\n548\tInspired by EVAR, we formulate performance-related tasks\n549\tin a unified package, EVPMR, providing standardised evaluation protocols. All audio-only datasets are transcribed\n550\tto MIDI with Transkun [39] for consistency. Every task\n551\tis evaluated with 5-fold cross-validation repeated across 5\n552\trandom seeds. On top of a frozen global embedding from\n553\tencode(), classification uses a linear probe, pairwise\n554\tranking uses a binary classification over concatenated pair\n555\tfeatures, regression uses Ridge, and UMP uses a framelevel MLP decoder.\n556\tPerformance assessment. Three datasets provide normalised ratings in [0, 1]: NeuroPiano [4] (104 recordings,\n557\t27 students, 6 pieces, rated by 33 teachers on a 0–6 scale),\n558\tYCU-PPE-III [43] (606 performances, 13 songs, rated by 3\n559\tinstructors on a 0–100 scale), and PISA [44] (59 YouTube\n560\tvideos, skill level 1–10). Ratings are aggregated per recording and normalised using min–max scaling. We report\n561\tMAE↓ as the primary metric.\n562\tChopin competition ranking. Following PianoJudge [5], this task uses 130 preliminary-round performances from the 2015 International Chopin Piano Competition, each labelled with the round the performer reached\n563\t(0–4). We cast it as pairwise ranking: for every ordered\n564\tpair of performances with different labels, the probe predicts which of the two advanced further. We report pairwise\n565\tranking accuracy (chance = 0.50).\n566\tTechnique detection. From the Pianism-Labelling\n567\tDataset [5] we use the single-label piano technique classification task, which assigns each performance excerpt to one\n568\tof 7 technique categories (octaves, scales, etc.).\n569\tPianist8. Introduced with MidiBERT-Piano [21], this\n570\tdataset contains 411 pieces by 8 pianists spanning four\n571\tstylistic categories. The task is 8-way pianist identification,\n572\ttesting whether representations capture individual styles.\n573\tUnreferenced Mistake Prediction (UMP). Following\n574\tthe score-free “conspicuous mistake” formulation introduced by [1], UMP detects performance errors from musical context alone. We evaluate on three open-source\n575\tdatasets: EN-augmented (EN) [45], Burgmüller (BM) [1],\n576\tand ScoreInformed (SI) [46]. UMP is token-level: we\n577\textract per-token embeddings, assign each token a timestamp and duration, and group them into a fixed 30 ms grid\n578\t(duration-aware: each token contributes to all frames it\n579\t\n580\t\fTable 2. Downstream evaluation on the EVPMR benchmark. Mean (std.) over 5-fold cross-validation across 5 random\n581\tseeds.\n582\tUMP: F 1\n583\t\n584\tChopin\n585\t\n586\tTechnique\n587\t\n588\tModel\n589\t\n590\tPairAcc\n591\t\n592\tAcc\n593\t\n594\tPISA\n595\t\n596\tQuality Regression: MAE↓\n597\tNeuroPiano\n598\t\n599\tYCU-PPE\n600\t\n601\tEN\n602\t\n603\tBM\n604\t\n605\tAria\n606\tMoonbeam\n607\tMusicBERT\n608\tCLaMP 3\n609\t\n610\t47.5 (8.1)\n611\t53.7 (2.9)\n612\t52.5 (5.8)\n613\t53.5 (8.5)\n614\t\n615\t66.2 (11.4)\n616\t77.9 (4.8)\n617\t67.1 (4.6)\n618\t70.3 (2.7)\n619\t\n620\t.122 (.031)\n621\t.151 (.035)\n622\t.190 (.026)\n623\t.217 (.036)\n624\t\n625\t.215 (.019)\n626\t.192 (.014)\n627\t.205 (.019)\n628\t.203 (.017)\n629\t\n630\t.068 (.005)\n631\t.061 (.004)\n632\t.065 (.004)\n633\t.089 (.004)\n634\t\n635\t.174 (.025)\n636\t–\n637\t–\n638\t–\n639\t\n640\t.313 (.016)\n641\t–\n642\t–\n643\t–\n644\t\n645\tMAJEPPA (Lpiece )\n646\t53.7 (8.1)\n647\tMAJEPPA (Lpiece +Lcond )\n648\t58.7 (8.7)\n649\tMAJEPPA (Lpiece +Lcond +Lself ) 59.7 (9.4)\n650\t\n651\t76.3 (5.9)\n652\t70.3 (9.5)\n653\t79.0 (6.5)\n654\t\n655\t.125 (.083)\n656\t.108 (.090)\n657\t.090 (.034)\n658\t\n659\t.180 (.015)\n660\t.181 (.022)\n661\t.155 (.018)\n662\t\n663\t.064 (.004) .151 (.021) .308 (.012) .313 (.015) 83.1 (3.2)\n664\t.062 (.005) .141 (.015) .321 (.012) .341 (.004) 86.8 (5.2)\n665\t.059 (.002) .179 (.012) .333 (.015) .316 (.037) 84.2 (3.8)\n666\t\n667\tspans). A per-frame MLP probe predicts binary mistake\n668\tlabels; predictions are merged into segments and evaluated\n669\twith mir_eval.transcription (segment F 1, 2 s tolerance). As this protocol relies on the model’s tokenisation\n670\tand onset/duration information, we therefore compare MAJEPPA only against the Aria baseline on UMP as other\n671\tbaselines use incomparable token schemes.\n672\tResults: As reported in Table 2, the full MAJEPPA\n673\tmodel attains the best score on Chopin, Technique, all regression tasks, and UMP EN/BM, while the Lpiece +Lcond\n674\tvariant is best on Pianist8 and UMP SI. MAJEPPA variants exceeds four baselines on Chopin, Technique, and the\n675\tregression tasks, indicating that score-conditioned JEPA\n676\tpre-training captures expressive and pedagogical structure not recovered by purely generative (Aria, Moonbeam)\n677\tor masked-reconstruction (MusicBERT, CLaMP 3) pretraining. Aria’s tokenizer has no explicit pedal tokens (sustain is folded into note durations), so the Aria baseline\n678\tcomparison does not isolate pedalling as a discrete cue.\n679\tThe ablation reveals a division of labour among the three\n680\tlosses. Lpiece alone is already competitive with the strongest\n681\tbaselines on most tasks. Adding Lcond benefits the tagdiscriminative tasks (Pianist8, UMP SI), where samples\n682\twith different but similar condition tags are brought closer\n683\taccording to wij . Adding Lself further reduces regression\n684\tMAE and improves Chopin and UMP EN/BM, but slightly\n685\tdegrades Pianist8 and UMP SI: pulling within-performance\n686\tsegments together smooths over the segment-level cues\n687\tthese two tasks rely on.\n688\t5.1 Generation and embedding space analysis\n689\tDue to time constraints, we did not conduct a full-scale evaluation of the generation capabilities. However, we invite\n690\treaders to visit our website 1 for sample outputs and interactive generation. In our demo website, we also showcase\n691\trepresentative trajectories of PCA-reduced embeddings.\n692\tPractice progression. On the session-averaged embeddings of 7 YouTube practice journeys, we measure Spearman rank correlations between session index (from earliest\n693\tto latest session) and the trajectory’s leading principal component. The mean correlation is ρ = 0.41 across PC1 and\n694\tPC2, with trajectories exhibiting strong monotonic drift.\n695\tEven in cases with moderate correlation, the latest-session\n696\tpoints consistently concentrate at one end of each trajectory cloud (Figure 4), suggesting that MAJEPPA induces\n697\ta directional notion of progress without explicit temporal\n698\t\n699\tPianist8\n700\tSI\n701\t\n702\tAcc\n703\t\n704\t.300 (.020) 86.1 (4.3)\n705\t–\n706\t80.6 (4.5)\n707\t–\n708\t78.6 (2.1)\n709\t–\n710\t80.8 (3.3)\n711\t\n712\tFigure 4. Samples from embedding viewer. Left: Practice\n713\ttrajectories for 30 days of Rachmaninov practice, coloured\n714\twhite (early) to blue (late). Right: UMAP of mistake\n715\tregions for Burgmüller Étude Op. 100 No. 17 with annotations coloured by canonical mistake type, matching the\n716\tinteractive website.\n717\tsupervision.\n718\tMistake topology. Across the 25 Burgmüller études,\n719\twe project mistake-annotated 4-second windows to 3D via\n720\tUMAP, and further label 94 of the annotated regions with\n721\tcategorical mistake types (mistouch, wrong-pitch, rollback,\n722\t. . . ) drawn from independent human annotations in the\n723\tSynMist project [2]. Colouring by mistake type (Figure 4,\n724\tright) reveals contiguous within-piece subregions: timingdisruption mistakes (rollback / hands-off / hold) sit apart\n725\tfrom pitch-deviation mistakes (mistouch / wrong-pitch),\n726\tsuggesting that MAJEPPA encodes the texture of a deviation rather than a single \"is-a-mistake\" axis.\n727\t6. CONCLUSION\n728\tWe presented MAJEPPA, a dataset and framework for unified piano performance representations. We contribute\n729\t∼4,000 annotated recordings across six expertise levels\n730\tand six recording contexts, spanning from child beginners\n731\tto concert virtuosi. We use the dataset to train an LLMJEPA model for symbolic music using a joint objective\n732\tcombining score-conditioned generation, cross-condition\n733\tclustering, and within-performance alignment. MAJEPPA\n734\testablishes a new representation space for music education,\n735\twhere performance quality, progression, and technique can\n736\tbe modelled, compared, and guided in a unified and datadriven manner.\n737\t\n738\t\f7. ETHICS STATEMENT\n739\tMAJEPPA is built from publicly available YouTube solopiano recordings for research purposes; because these\n740\trecordings are publicly accessible, we did not obtain individual consent from uploaders. The corpus includes children’s\n741\tpractice and showcase videos and commercial or concert\n742\trecordings by professional pianists. However, public availability does not constitute a waiver of the uploaders’ or\n743\tperformers’ underlying rights. To mitigate this, we release\n744\tonly derived MIDI and coarse descriptive tags (expertise\n745\tand recording context), not raw audio or personally identifying metadata beyond what is already public, and we\n746\twill respond promptly to any request from an uploader, performer, or rights holder to remove specific content from the\n747\tdataset. Additionally, we intend the resource for research\n748\ton performance understanding and music education rather\n749\tthan for replacing teachers or performers.\n750\t8. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n751\tThis work is supported by the UKRI Centre for Doctoral\n752\tTraining in Artificial Intelligence and Music. J. Zhou is a\n753\tresearch student supported jointly by the China Scholarship\n754\tCouncil and Queen Mary University of London.\n755\t9. REFERENCES\n756\t[1] A. Morsi, K. Tatsumi, A. Maezawa, T. Fujishima, and\n757\tX. Serra, “Sounds out of pläce? Score-Independent detection of conspicuous mistakes in piano performances,”\n758\tin Proceedings of the International Society for Music\n759\tInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2023, pp.\n760\t352–358.\n761\t[2] A. Morsi, H. Zhang, A. Maezawa, S. Dixon, and\n762\tX. Serra, “Simulating piano performance mistakes for\n763\tmusic learning,” in Proceedings of the 21st Sound and\n764\tMusic Computing Conference (SMC), 2024, pp. 179–\n765\t186.\n766\t[3] S. Chang, S. Dixon, and E. Benetos, “RUMAA:\n767\tRepeat-aware unified music audio analysis for scoreperformance alignment, transcription, and mistake detection,” in 2025 IEEE Workshop on Applications of\n768\tSignal Processing to Audio and Acoustics (WASPAA),\n769\t2025, pp. 1–5.\n770\t[4] H. Zhang, V. Cheung, H. Nishioka, S. Dixon, and S. Furuya, “How does the teacher rate? Observations from\n771\tthe NeuroPiano dataset,” in International Society for\n772\tMusic Information Retrieval (ISMIR) Late Breaking\n773\tDemo (LBD), 2024.\n774\t[5] H. Zhang, J. Liang, and S. Dixon, “From audio encoders to piano judges: Benchmarking performance\n775\tunderstanding for solo piano,” in Proceedings of the\n776\t25th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2024, pp. 511–519.\n777\t[6] H. Zhang, V. K. Cheung, H. Nishioka, S. Dixon, and\n778\tS. Furuya, “LLaQo: Towards a query-based coach in\n779\t\n780\texpressive music performance assessment,” in ICASSP\n781\t2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025, pp.\n782\t1–5.\n783\t[7] Š. Libřický and J. Hajič, “Modeling the difficulty of saxophone music,” in Proceedings of the 26th International\n784\tSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025, pp. 747–754.\n785\t[8] M. Müllerschon, A. Klapuri, M. Rodriguez, and\n786\tC. Cardin, “Playability prediction in digital guitar learning using interpretable student and song representations,”\n787\tin Proceedings of the 26th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025,\n788\tpp. 631–637.\n789\t[9] Z. Hassein-Bey, Y. Abbou, A. D’Hooge, M. Giraud,\n790\tG. Guillemain, and A. Jeanneau, “What song now?\n791\tPersonalized rhythm guitar learning in western popular\n792\tmusic,” in Proceedings of the 26th International Society\n793\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR),\n794\t2025, pp. 296–302.\n795\t[10] A. Morsi, S. Chiruthapudi, S. D. Peter, M. Pilkov,\n796\tL. Bishop, A. Maezawa, X. Serra, and C. E. CancinoChacón, “Enabling empirical analysis of piano performance rehearsal with the Rach3 MIDI dataset,” in Proceedings of the 26th International Society for Music\n797\tInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025, pp.\n798\t484–491.\n799\t[11] Y. Kim, J. Park, J. Bae, K. Kim, T. Kwon, A. Lerch,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6794b662-7f11-40e1-a8da-8cda3a766949", "uuid": "4186af61-930a-4ef6-af13-5ba95aaeef33", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T09:26:35.865Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/majeppa.txt", "content": "j∈Ci\n\n(3)\nP\nwith w̄ij = wij / h∈Ci wih row-normalised over Ci . This\nterm replaces the cosine-distance loss of [16], which we\nfound prone to representation collapse in our setting. See\nour website 1 for details of continuous condition similarity\ndesign.\nWithin-performance alignment Lself is a symmetric\nInfoNCE loss over pairs of segments from the same performance. We form B pairs (pai , pbi ) with embeddings zai , zbi ,\nand define\nLself = −\n\nB\n1 X X\nexp(zui ·ziū /τ )\n,\nlog PB\nu ū\nB i=1\nj=1 exp(zi ·zj /τ )\n\n(4)\n\nu∈{a,b}\n\nwhere ā = b and b̄ = a. Both terms encourage segments\nfrom the same performance to be more similar than segments from different recordings, while averaging over the\ntwo directions yields a symmetric objective. Since the two\nsegments share performer and recording tags, Lself complements Lcond by encouraging within-performance coherence.\nTraining setup: We freeze the pre-trained Aria weights\nand apply Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [41] to the attention projections mixed_qkv and att_proj_linear\n\n(rank r = 512, scaling αLoRA = 32), giving ∼75M trainable\nparameters out of 660M. The context window is extended\nfrom 2048 to 4096 tokens via linear RoPE scaling [42].\nLoss hyperparameters in Eq. (1) are set to γ = 1, λ = 8,\nβ = 1, α = 1, with temperature τ = 0.07 and k = 1 [PRED]\ntoken, chosen by ablation.\nWe train on 4×NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs in bfloat16\nmixed precision for 120 epochs, with a per-GPU batch size\nof 8 and gradient accumulation of 4. Optimisation uses\nAdamW (weight decay 0.01) with separate learning rates\nfor these LoRA modules (2×10−4 ), the new conditioning\nand [PRED] token embeddings (1×10−3 ), and the LM\nhead (1×10−5 ).\n5. DOWNSTREAM TASKS AND EVALUATION\nInspired by EVAR, we formulate performance-related tasks\nin a unified package, EVPMR, providing standardised evaluation protocols. All audio-only datasets are transcribed\nto MIDI with Transkun [39] for consistency. Every task\nis evaluated with 5-fold cross-validation repeated across 5\nrandom seeds. On top of a frozen global embedding from\nencode(), classification uses a linear probe, pairwise\nranking uses a binary classification over concatenated pair\nfeatures, regression uses Ridge, and UMP uses a framelevel MLP decoder.\nPerformance assessment. Three datasets provide normalised ratings in [0, 1]: NeuroPiano [4] (104 recordings,\n27 students, 6 pieces, rated by 33 teachers on a 0–6 scale),\nYCU-PPE-III [43] (606 performances, 13 songs, rated by 3\ninstructors on a 0–100 scale), and PISA [44] (59 YouTube\nvideos, skill level 1–10). Ratings are aggregated per recording and normalised using min–max scaling. We report\nMAE↓ as the primary metric.\nChopin competition ranking. Following PianoJudge [5], this task uses 130 preliminary-round performances from the 2015 International Chopin Piano Competition, each labelled with the round the performer reached\n(0–4). We cast it as pairwise ranking: for every ordered\npair of performances with different labels, the probe predicts which of the two advanced further. We report pairwise\nranking accuracy (chance = 0.50).\nTechnique detection. From the Pianism-Labelling\nDataset [5] we use the single-label piano technique classification task, which assigns each performance excerpt to one\nof 7 technique categories (octaves, scales, etc.).\nPianist8. Introduced with MidiBERT-Piano [21], this\ndataset contains 411 pieces by 8 pianists spanning four\nstylistic categories. The task is 8-way pianist identification,\ntesting whether representations capture individual styles.\nUnreferenced Mistake Prediction (UMP). Following\nthe score-free “conspicuous mistake” formulation introduced by [1], UMP detects performance errors from musical context alone. We evaluate on three open-source\ndatasets: EN-augmented (EN) [45], Burgmüller (BM) [1],\nand ScoreInformed (SI) [46]. UMP is token-level: we\nextract per-token embeddings, assign each token a timestamp and duration, and group them into a fixed 30 ms grid\n(duration-aware: each token contributes to all frames it\n\n\fTable 2. Downstream evaluation on the EVPMR benchmark. Mean (std.) over 5-fold cross-validation across 5 random\nseeds.\nUMP: F 1\n\nChopin\n\nTechnique\n\nModel\n\nPairAcc\n\nAcc\n\nPISA\n\nQuality Regression: MAE↓\nNeuroPiano\n\nYCU-PPE\n\nEN\n\nBM\n\nAria\nMoonbeam\nMusicBERT\nCLaMP 3\n\n47.5 (8.1)\n53.7 (2.9)\n52.5 (5.8)\n53.5 (8.5)\n\n66.2 (11.4)\n77.9 (4.8)\n67.1 (4.6)\n70.3 (2.7)\n\n.122 (.031)\n.151 (.035)\n.190 (.026)\n.217 (.036)\n\n.215 (.019)\n.192 (.014)\n.205 (.019)\n.203 (.017)\n\n.068 (.005)\n.061 (.004)\n.065 (.004)\n.089 (.004)\n\n.174 (.025)\n–\n–\n–\n\n.313 (.016)\n–\n–\n–\n\nMAJEPPA (Lpiece )\n53.7 (8.1)\nMAJEPPA (Lpiece +Lcond )\n58.7 (8.7)\nMAJEPPA (Lpiece +Lcond +Lself ) 59.7 (9.4)\n\n76.3 (5.9)\n70.3 (9.5)\n79.0 (6.5)\n\n.125 (.083)\n.108 (.090)\n.090 (.034)\n\n.180 (.015)\n.181 (.022)\n.155 (.018)\n\n.064 (.004) .151 (.021) .308 (.012) .313 (.015) 83.1 (3.2)\n.062 (.005) .141 (.015) .321 (.012) .341 (.004) 86.8 (5.2)\n.059 (.002) .179 (.012) .333 (.015) .316 (.037) 84.2 (3.8)\n\nspans). A per-frame MLP probe predicts binary mistake\nlabels; predictions are merged into segments and evaluated\nwith mir_eval.transcription (segment F 1, 2 s tolerance). As this protocol relies on the model’s tokenisation\nand onset/duration information, we therefore compare MAJEPPA only against the Aria baseline on UMP as other\nbaselines use incomparable token schemes.\nResults: As reported in Table 2, the full MAJEPPA\nmodel attains the best score on Chopin, Technique, all regression tasks, and UMP EN/BM, while the Lpiece +Lcond\nvariant is best on Pianist8 and UMP SI. MAJEPPA variants exceeds four baselines on Chopin, Technique, and the\nregression tasks, indicating that score-conditioned JEPA\npre-training captures expressive and pedagogical structure not recovered by purely generative (Aria, Moonbeam)\nor masked-reconstruction (MusicBERT, CLaMP 3) pretraining. Aria’s tokenizer has no explicit pedal tokens (sustain is folded into note durations), so the Aria baseline\ncomparison does not isolate pedalling as a discrete cue.\nThe ablation reveals a division of labour among the three\nlosses. Lpiece alone is already competitive with the strongest\nbaselines on most tasks. Adding Lcond benefits the tagdiscriminative tasks (Pianist8, UMP SI), where samples\nwith different but similar condition tags are brought closer\naccording to wij . Adding Lself further reduces regression\nMAE and improves Chopin and UMP EN/BM, but slightly\ndegrades Pianist8 and UMP SI: pulling within-performance\nsegments together smooths over the segment-level cues\nthese two tasks rely on.\n5.1 Generation and embedding space analysis\nDue to time constraints, we did not conduct a full-scale evaluation of the generation capabilities. However, we invite\nreaders to visit our website 1 for sample outputs and interactive generation. In our demo website, we also showcase\nrepresentative trajectories of PCA-reduced embeddings.\nPractice progression. On the session-averaged embeddings of 7 YouTube practice journeys, we measure Spearman rank correlations between session index (from earliest\nto latest session) and the trajectory’s leading principal component. The mean correlation is ρ = 0.41 across PC1 and\nPC2, with trajectories exhibiting strong monotonic drift.\nEven in cases with moderate correlation, the latest-session\npoints consistently concentrate at one end of each trajectory cloud (Figure 4), suggesting that MAJEPPA induces\na directional notion of progress without explicit temporal\n\nPianist8\nSI\n\nAcc\n\n.300 (.020) 86.1 (4.3)\n–\n80.6 (4.5)\n–\n78.6 (2.1)\n–\n80.8 (3.3)\n\nFigure 4. Samples from embedding viewer. Left: Practice\ntrajectories for 30 days of Rachmaninov practice, coloured\nwhite (early) to blue (late). Right: UMAP of mistake\nregions for Burgmüller Étude Op. 100 No. 17 with annotations coloured by canonical mistake type, matching the\ninteractive website.\nsupervision.\nMistake topology. Across the 25 Burgmüller études,\nwe project mistake-annotated 4-second windows to 3D via\nUMAP, and further label 94 of the annotated regions with\ncategorical mistake types (mistouch, wrong-pitch, rollback,\n. . . ) drawn from independent human annotations in the\nSynMist project [2]. Colouring by mistake type (Figure 4,\nright) reveals contiguous within-piece subregions: timingdisruption mistakes (rollback / hands-off / hold) sit apart\nfrom pitch-deviation mistakes (mistouch / wrong-pitch),\nsuggesting that MAJEPPA encodes the texture of a deviation rather than a single \"is-a-mistake\" axis.\n6. CONCLUSION\nWe presented MAJEPPA, a dataset and framework for unified piano performance representations. We contribute\n∼4,000 annotated recordings across six expertise levels\nand six recording contexts, spanning from child beginners\nto concert virtuosi. We use the dataset to train an LLMJEPA model for symbolic music using a joint objective\ncombining score-conditioned generation, cross-condition\nclustering, and within-performance alignment. MAJEPPA\nestablishes a new representation space for music education,\nwhere performance quality, progression, and technique can\nbe modelled, compared, and guided in a unified and datadriven manner.\n\n\f7. ETHICS STATEMENT\nMAJEPPA is built from publicly available YouTube solopiano recordings for research purposes; because these\nrecordings are publicly accessible, we did not obtain individual consent from uploaders. The corpus includes children’s\npractice and showcase videos and commercial or concert\nrecordings by professional pianists. However, public availability does not constitute a waiver of the uploaders’ or\nperformers’ underlying rights. To mitigate this, we release\nonly derived MIDI and coarse descriptive tags (expertise\nand recording context), not raw audio or personally identifying metadata beyond what is already public, and we\nwill respond promptly to any request from an uploader, performer, or rights holder to remove specific content from the\ndataset. Additionally, we intend the resource for research\non performance understanding and music education rather\nthan for replacing teachers or performers.\n8. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nThis work is supported by the UKRI Centre for Doctoral\nTraining in Artificial Intelligence and Music. J. Zhou is a\nresearch student supported jointly by the China Scholarship\nCouncil and Queen Mary University of London.\n9. REFERENCES\n[1] A. Morsi, K. Tatsumi, A. Maezawa, T. Fujishima, and\nX. Serra, “Sounds out of pläce? Score-Independent detection of conspicuous mistakes in piano performances,”\nin Proceedings of the International Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2023, pp.\n352–358.\n[2] A. Morsi, H. Zhang, A. Maezawa, S. Dixon, and\nX. Serra, “Simulating piano performance mistakes for\nmusic learning,” in Proceedings of the 21st Sound and\nMusic Computing Conference (SMC), 2024, pp. 179–\n186.\n[3] S. Chang, S. Dixon, and E. Benetos, “RUMAA:\nRepeat-aware unified music audio analysis for scoreperformance alignment, transcription, and mistake detection,” in 2025 IEEE Workshop on Applications of\nSignal Processing to Audio and Acoustics (WASPAA),\n2025, pp. 1–5.\n[4] H. Zhang, V. Cheung, H. Nishioka, S. Dixon, and S. Furuya, “How does the teacher rate? Observations from\nthe NeuroPiano dataset,” in International Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval (ISMIR) Late Breaking\nDemo (LBD), 2024.\n[5] H. Zhang, J. Liang, and S. Dixon, “From audio encoders to piano judges: Benchmarking performance\nunderstanding for solo piano,” in Proceedings of the\n25th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2024, pp. 511–519.\n[6] H. Zhang, V. K. Cheung, H. Nishioka, S. Dixon, and\nS. Furuya, “LLaQo: Towards a query-based coach in\n\nexpressive music performance assessment,” in ICASSP\n2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025, pp.\n1–5.\n[7] Š. Libřický and J. Hajič, “Modeling the difficulty of saxophone music,” in Proceedings of the 26th International\nSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025, pp. 747–754.\n[8] M. Müllerschon, A. Klapuri, M. Rodriguez, and\nC. Cardin, “Playability prediction in digital guitar learning using interpretable student and song representations,”\nin Proceedings of the 26th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025,\npp. 631–637.\n[